01:24:28,252 --> 01:24:54,307
Good evening. I'm really pleased to be here tonight to share another exciting step forward for career and technical education at Medford High School. I want to first recognize how much our CTE programming continues to grow. With the recent Chapter 74 approval of plumbing and information support services and networking, we now offer 17 state-approved CTE programs at Medford High.

24
01:24:54,644 --> 01:25:12,329
And that's something I'm extremely proud of, and it means more opportunities and more pathways for our students. You'll also hear us using the word major much more intentionally this year. Each of these 17 CTE programs represents a student's major. They aren't electives or simply classes students take.

25
01:25:12,329 --> 01:25:32,579
Our students spend approximately 50% of their high school experience in their CTE major developing technical skills, completing rigorous coursework, earning industry credentials, and gaining real-world experience. As these programs continue to grow, I think it's equally important that we continue to recognize the rigor and achievement within them.

26
01:25:33,203 --> 01:26:00,845
That brings us to what I'm really excited to share tonight, our new career tech education honors pilot for the 2026-27 school year. We're beginning with engineering, health assisting, film and television production, and programming and web development. Honors will be optional, earned, and performance-based, with students demonstrating that higher level of achievement through either cooperative education or a rigorous capstone experience.

27
01:26:01,030 --> 01:26:18,361
We're also continuing to look for ways to strengthen the academic experiences connected to all of our CTE majors. One example this year is the addition of Design 1 for our Graphic Design and Visual Communication students, which gives them a stronger foundation in the principles of design and complements the technical work they're already doing.

28
01:26:19,508 --> 01:26:49,208
I'm really proud of the direction we're moving in, and as our CTE programs continue to grow, we want our students to see their CTE major as a rigorous, challenging, and important part of their future. Whether that future is college, career, apprenticeship, union, or further training, we support our students. So with that, I'd like to walk you through the honors pilot and what we hope to accomplish this year. And I do just want to say a special thank you to Dr. Talbot for her support and guidance through this project.

29
01:26:56,414 --> 01:27:17,204
So the vision for CTE is to be a defining part of the Medford High School experience, where students discover their strengths, develop real-world skills, and prepare for successful futures. A student's CTE program is their major, a rigorous pathway that combines academics, hands-on learning, credentials, and real-world experiences.

30
01:27:17,575 --> 01:27:46,583
Whether the next step is college, career, apprenticeship, union, military, or further training, our goal is simple. Every CTE student graduates with skills, options, and a plan for what comes next. I'm going to take this one. OK. Per the Department of Education guidance, Chapter 74 cooperative education and approved post-secondary articulation are considered advanced coursework, comparable to AP, IB, and dual enrollment.

31
01:27:47,157 --> 01:28:12,200
So in Medford, students who successfully complete a cooperative education experience are already meeting this high bar and are reported to the state and completed advanced coursework. But through the years, for a variety of reasons, not all students who have the prerequisite skills to engage in co-op are able to do so. So despite this reporting structure, the grades students earn are assigned a standard weighting.

32
01:28:16,975 --> 01:28:41,495
The challenge is that CTE students, as you probably know, spend approximately 50% of their school day in technical instruction. Advanced coursework currently receives standard GPA weighting, so all of our programs now receive standard weighting. And our students have fewer opportunities to earn weighted GPA than their peers that are enrolled in honors or AP courses.

33
01:28:42,490 --> 01:29:09,524
And this does create a structural integrity to student performance, unrelated to student performance. Coincidentally enough, in June 2026, Governor Healey affirmed recommendations for modernizing high school graduation requirements. And one of those components is a capstone project. So Medford was ahead of the curve once again. So the options are as follows.

34
01:29:10,621 --> 01:29:31,259
Students are gonna have two opportunities to earn honors in the four programs that we're piloting this year. Option one is through cooperative education, which we already do. Last year, we had about 65 of our 125 students who graduated career tech ed employed up until the day they graduated, and most students went off to work the day after graduation.

35
01:29:32,626 --> 01:29:56,791
Option two is a capstone project. You can think of it as a thesis. You can think of it as something the students will defend. Ultimately, it's a portfolio. It's related to their CTE major. We're gonna bring in industry professionals, most likely from our cooperative employers or our advisory committee to come in and judge the student's portfolio. And they can use this on their next step after high school, whether it's for employment or college placement.

36
01:29:59,339 --> 01:30:23,774
The selection criteria was that we, you know, we looked at, we had to start somewhere. So the four that we picked, they have a strong post-secondary alignment, they have articulation agreements, we have partnerships with not only employers but with schools. They have project and portfolio-based learning. They have the capacity to support this work and their college and career readiness for each program.

37
01:30:25,090 --> 01:30:52,006
It's also important to mention that this didn't just begin with an idea. There were students who came to see me. It began with our engineering students who wanted more out of the opportunities we had. They wanted an opportunity to learn to earn more credits above standard. It also was re-instilled by a belief that we all share that the work these students are doing is far above standard level.

38
01:30:52,192 --> 01:31:19,630
A quick example, we have students at Mass General Hospital, we have students in medical offices. This is work that students are doing that I think we could all agree is already above standard level, so they should earn credit for that. So the four programs that we're piloting for this year is our engineering program, health assisting, film and television production, and programming and web development. The honors component is optional, it's earned, and it's performance-based.

39
01:31:21,959 --> 01:31:45,955
